AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are changing how graduate students search for knowledge, write code, debug programs, summarize literature, and build AI applications. To respond to this change, this paper redesigns the graduate course Principles and Applications of Artificial Intelligence around a large-model-centered teaching framework. The framework retains classical AI foundations while adding Transformer principles, pre-training, prompt engineering, API-based practice, lightweight agents, model evaluation, and responsible AI use. Project tasks are organized around research and engineering scenarios so that students learn to use LLMs as controllable technical tools rather than only as answer generators. To provide preliminary classroom evidence, a 30-item self-report questionnaire was conducted with 300 valid responses from current and former graduate students. The items covered fundamental AI concepts, Transformer and large-model principles, and application and engineering topics. Each item used a five-point Likert-type scale to measure respondents' perceived understanding, recognition, and application confidence. The overall mean score was 4.09 out of 5, and the overall highscore rate was 79.01%. Respondents gave higher ratings to fundamental AI concepts (mean = 4.21; high-score rate = 84.67%) and application and engineering topics (mean = 4.20; high-score rate = 82.90%) than to Transformer and large-model principles $(\text{mean}=3.87; \text{high-score rate}=69.47\%)$. The results indicate that LLM-centered teaching is positively perceived in graduate AI education when it combines theory reconstruction, engineering practice, output verification, and ethical reflection, while more targeted instruction is still needed for large-model mechanisms and reliability issues.
ER  - 

