AB  - Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have enabled multi-agent systems capable of tackling complex tasks. Nevertheless, the performance of these systems is often limited by rigid, manually crafted “agentic workflows” that govern agent interactions. Such workflows are either too simplistic to handle complex problems or unnecessarily complex for simpler tasks, resulting in high computational costs and latency. To address these limitations, we introduce EvoRL, a novel framework that leverages hierarchical reinforcement learning to automate the construction of agentic workflows. EvoRL frames workflow design as a sequential decision-making process, learning adaptive policies to dynamically assemble workflows tailored to each query. The framework employs a high-level Evolver agent to select modular agentic components that define the workflow structure, and a low-level LLM Selector agent to choose the most cost-effective language model for each component. Empirical validation of EvoRL on benchmarks including GSM8K, MATH, MultiArith, HumanEval, and MBPP demonstrates that EvoRL achieves up to 87.99 % average accuracy, surpassing static baselines such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) at $\text{8 0. 4 5} \%$ as well as stochastic methods. In addition, EvoRL reduces the average number of episodes required to reach a solution by 38% and substantially lowers computational costs. These findings highlight EvoRL's robust generalization across both mathematical reasoning and code generation domains.
