AB  - The rapid progress of large language models (LLMs) has transformed conversational AI, yet challenges persist in evaluating voice-enabled assistants with respect to privacy, latency, inclusivity, and reproducibility. This study introduces Donna, a locally deployed assistant powered by open-source models (Mistral family) and benchmarks it against commercial counterparts ChatGPT and Gemini, with GitHub Copilot planned for code-specific baselines. A balanced 12-prompt suite spanning summarisation, factual QA, reasoning, paraphrasing, code generation, and dialectal tasks (Yorùbá, Nigerian Pidgin, Hausa) was employed. Evaluation integrates automatic metrics (BLEU, ROUGE-L, METEOR, Exact Match, Levenshtein distance, Word Error Rate), operational measures (stage-aware latency, token usage), and human-centred ratings (clarity, creativity, hallucination, and dialect responsiveness). Results show that BLEU/ROUGE-L saturate at ceiling values across all models, underscoring their limitations. Latency and verbosity emerge as discriminative factors: ChatGPT offers fastest responses $(\approx 2-6s)$, Gemini balances creativity with longer outputs $(\approx 5-45 ~\mathrm{s})$, while Donna incurs higher end-to-end latency $(\approx 120-150s)$ due to full local STT-LLM-TTS cycles. Human evaluation highlights complementary strengths: ChatGPT excels in clarity, Gemini in creativity, and Donna in dialectal support. External reviewers (educators and computer science students) validated Donna's promise in privacy-preserving and educational contexts, while noting translation limitations beyond target dialects. The study contributes a reproducible, voice-first evaluation framework with unified logging, stage-aware instrumentation, and hybrid metrics that combine automatic, operational, and human-centred perspectives. Findings underscore the trade-offs between speed, creativity, inclusivity, and privacy, and provide guidance for deploying ethical and dialect-sensitive assistants in multilingual, resource-constrained environments.
