AB  - The development of Internet of Things (IoT) systems is characterized by high technical complexity, involving cross-layer protocol stacks and invisible hardware states. Novice engineers often face significant challenges in debugging and system optimization due to the lack of context-aware guidance. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in code generation, their generic nature often leads to “hallucinations” or superficial answers when applied to specific embedded hardware contexts. To address this, this paper presents the design and implementation of a Context-Aware AI Scaffolding System. The system features a novel IDE plugin that automatically captures runtime context (e.g., compilation errors, register states, network topology) and integrates it with a “Role-ContextConstraint” (R-C-C) Prompt Engineering Framework. Furthermore, a dynamic fading algorithm is implemented to adaptively adjust the level of AI assistance. Experimental results from a study with 90 participants demonstrate that the system effectively assists users in complex engineering tasks: the experimental group achieved a 50% reduction in routing convergence time and a 47% improvement in energy efficiency compared to the control group. These findings validate the system's efficacy in bridging the gap between generic AI capabilities and domain-specific engineering requirements.
