AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated considerable proficiency in code generation yet falter when faced with competitive programming problems that demand deep algorithmic reasoning and optimal solutions. Traditional training paradigms like Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) often encourage imitation of training data rather than fostering the robust, flexible problem-solving skills required to navigate complex constraints. This paper introduces a novel training framework designed to bridge this gap by enabling LLMs to reason through complexity. Our approach begins with SFT using Chain-of-Thought (CoT) supervision to establish a baseline for structured reasoning. We then advance to a Reinforcement Learning (RL) phase using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), which is driven by a unique, multi-faceted execution-guided reward function. This function provides feedback based not only on functional correctness but also on algorithmic efficiency, penalizing excessive time and memory usage and assigning rewards based on specific execution verdicts. This methodology steers the model away from brute-force or suboptimal patterns toward generating efficient and logically sound algorithms. Our experiments demonstrate that this executionguided RL approach significantly enhances the model's ability to solve complex problems, yielding solutions that are both correct and algorithmically superior to those produced by baseline models. This work validates a promising path toward developing LLMs with genuine algorithmic thinking capabilities.
