AB  - Small models have less than 3 billion parameters, making them economical to use and deploy. However, their main issue lies in their inability to handle mathematical problems. These language models tend to get lost midway during calculations and make even the simplest calculation mistakes. This paper attempts to rectify this through two methods, which both utilize a relatively small model known as Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct. This approach entails dividing the task into smaller units without losing sight of its overall progress towards the solution. The iterative reorganization helped in improving the multi-step reasoning capability from 54% to 61.33%, mainly through tackling the challenge of Inference-Time Truncation. The second method is a unified hybrid solver that routes each incoming problem to the appropriate solving pipeline based on its type. For structured algebraic problems, it combines a multi-pass variable resolver with Program-Aided Language (PAL) generation and a confidence-gated ensemble, achieving 95% accuracy on linear algebra datasets. For natural language word problems, it employs a three-strategy ensemble of Few-Shot PAL, Declarative code generation, and Self-Consistency voting over $k=7$ samples, evaluated on the GSM8K benchmark. The routing decision is made via lightweight regex pattern matching, adding negligible overhead. Both methods run within 6GB VRAM on consumergrade hardware, requiring no additional infrastructure. The core lesson is that structured inference architecture and problem-type-aware routing can substitute for parameter scaling, recovering reasoning capability that SLMs inherently lack.
ER  - 

