AB  - Python-based Extract-Transform-Load (ETL) repositories evolve rapidly as data sources, schemas, and business requirements change, but documentation often lags behind implementation. This gap increases maintenance effort, slows onboarding, and makes design degradation harder to identify. We present AURA, a multi-agent retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) framework for documentation generation and maintainability analysis in Python ETL repositories. AURA combines abstract syntax tree (AST)-based extraction, repository-aware retrieval, static analysis, and four coordinated agents-Reasoner, Documenter, Refactor, and Orchestrator-to produce function-level docstrings, summaries, design-smell assessments, and refactoring suggestions. We evaluate AURA on 12 open-source ETL repositories comprising 413 functions and compare it against GPT-4 direct prompting, a retrieval-augmented single-agent baseline, and CodeT5. AURA achieves the strongest overall results, improving documentation overlap metrics (Token F1: 0.12 vs. 0.08-0.09; ROUGE-L: 0.11 vs. 0.07-0.08) and design-smell detection performance (F1: 0.85 vs. 0.73-0.75). An ablation study shows that repository-aware retrieval is the largest contributor to documentation quality, while agent decomposition further improves design analysis. We also discuss why lexical overlap metrics remain low in repository-scale code documentation tasks and provide a preliminary qualitative assessment of output usefulness and consistency. The results suggest that repository-aware, multi-agent code understanding can provide practical maintainability support for ETL systems. We frame potential sustainability implications cautiously: although improved maintainability may reduce rework over time, this study does not directly measure energy use or carbon impact.
ER  - 

