AB  - CodeQL constitutes a robust paradigm for semanticbased static analysis within software supply chain security; however, its broader application is hindered by the formidable learning curve associated with Datalog and its intricate standard library. While Large Language Models (LLMs) present automation potential, they are plagued by a “paradigm mismatch”, resulting in frequent API hallucinations and syntactic instability. Current methodologies relying on fine-tuning or multiagent architectures frequently encounter obstacles regarding knowledge latency and prohibitive inference costs. To mitigate these deficiencies, we introduce AutoQL, a streamlined, retrievalaugmented code generation framework. AutoQL reconfigures query generation through a pipeline comprising “Semantic Alignment - Retrieval Augmentation - Iterative Constraint”. The system incorporates three primary contributions: (1) Incremental Semantic Slicing, which employs AST differencing to extract precise Source-Sink characteristics while attenuating contextual noise; (2) A Dual-Path RAG mechanism that maps natural language logic to valid CodeQL library APIs to curb hallucinations; and (3) A Compiler Feedback Repair Loop that harnesses error diagnostics to iteratively resolve rigorous type constraints. Evaluations on the CWE-Bench-Java dataset reveal that AutoQL elevates the zero-shot compilation success rate from $\mathbf{1 2. 8 \%}$ (GPT4) to 68.4% and secures an F1-Score of 0.61. It rivals state-of-the-art fine-tuned models in detection efficacy while curtailing inference expenditures by approximately 72%, offering a highly available paradigm for automated vulnerability mining.
