AB  - Agentic AI extends conventional large language model (LLM)-based services by enabling autonomous planning, tool use, memory access, external application programming interface (API) invocation, code generation, and agent-to-agent collaboration. These capabilities increase the practical value of AI systems, but they also introduce security risks that cannot be sufficiently addressed by model-level filtering, one-time authentication, or perimeter-based access control alone. The OWASP Agentic AI Top 10 identifies risks such as goal manipulation, tool misuse, privilege abuse, supply chain compromise, unexpected code execution, memory poisoning, insecure inter-agent communication, cascading failures, human-agent trust exploitation, and rogue agents. Existing Agentic AI security approaches mainly focus on prompt/output filtering, tool allowlisting, isolated sandboxing, or general policy guidance. However, they do not sufficiently specify where enforcement points should be placed in the Agentic AI workflow or how runtime context should be evaluated before agents invoke tools, access memory, communicate with other agents, or transmit data externally. This paper proposes a Zero Trust-based security architecture for Agentic AI using Policy Enforcement Points (PEPs) and a Policy Decision Point (PDP). The proposed architecture places enforcement points across inbound validation, internal agent behavior, memory access, inter-agent communication, outbound action, and external API mediation. It also defines PDP decision attributes such as subject identity, source trustworthiness, code and tool integrity, requested privilege, data sensitivity, task context, andruntime state. By mapping Agentic AI risks to concrete PEP/PDP controls, this paper provides a structured architecture for preserving agent autonomy while limiting unsafe actions, contaminated context propagation, and unauthorized externalinteractions.
ER  - 

