AB  - Visual representations are fundamental to software development, particularly in conveying design intent. Vision-Language Models (VLMs) present opportunities to incorporate visual inputs into AI-driven software development workflows. However, their ability to interpret visual artifacts-especially mockup diagrams-remains underexplored. These artifacts were originally designed for human communication, and are not necessarily optimized for VLMs. This study investigates whether VLMs can reason about design intent from mockup diagrams that implicitly encode requirements and functionality. We also examine how supplementary inputs, including state transitions, textual explanations, visual prompting, and example images, affect code generation quality. Through a preliminary experiment involving a simple puzzle game, we evaluated VLMs’ ability to implement UI, logic, and playability under various input conditions. Our findings suggest that while supplementary inputs improve output quality, mockup diagrams can provide adequate context for generating functional code.
ER  - 

